# What must never enter a build context.
#
# Two jobs. The first is speed: `node_modules` and `dist` are hundreds of
# megabytes that the build regenerates anyway, and every byte here is uploaded
# to the daemon before the first instruction runs.
#
# The second is the one that matters. **A build context is not a layer, but
# anything COPYed from it becomes one** — and a layer is extractable by anyone
# who can pull the image. `secrets/` holds JWT keys and Firebase service
# accounts; `.env` holds real credentials. Both are gitignored, which protects
# the repository and does nothing at all for an image build.

node_modules
**/node_modules
dist
**/dist
coverage
**/coverage
.turbo
**/.turbo

# **Both patterns.** A bare `*.tsbuildinfo` matches only the repo root, so the
# nested ones (`libs/common/tsconfig.build.tsbuildinfo`) were copied into the
# build context while `dist/` was excluded — and `tsc --incremental` then reads
# a buildinfo saying "everything is up to date", emits NOTHING, and the next
# package fails with "has no exported member" for every symbol in the library
# that was just built successfully.
*.tsbuildinfo
**/*.tsbuildinfo

# Credentials. See above — gitignore does not apply to a build context.
secrets/
**/secrets/
.env
**/.env
**/.env.*

# Python local state, so a developer's virtualenv never lands in an image.
.venv
**/.venv
__pycache__
**/__pycache__
.pytest_cache
.ruff_cache

.git
.github
docs
.vscode
.claude
